.\"
.\" The version comes from PROJECT_VERSION at configure time. There are already
.\" two places a release number has to be right -- CMakeLists.txt and the
.\" AppStream metainfo -- and a third that has to be remembered by hand is a
.\" third that will one day be wrong.
.TH FRETWORK 1 "2026-08-29" "fretwork @PROJECT_VERSION@" "Fretwork Manual"
.SH NAME
fretwork \- tablature editor and player that gives every track its own stem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fretwork
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fretwork
reads Guitar Pro files
.RI ( .gp )
and Fretwork's own scores
.RI ( .fw ),
draws them as tablature, plays them, and writes each track out as its own
audio stem. Every track has a synthesiser of its own, so soloing, muting and
stem export need no re-render and no bounce.
.PP
Given a file and nothing in particular to do with it, it opens a window.
Given
.BR \-\-info ,
.BR \-\-midi ,
.BR \-\-stems ,
.BR \-\-render ,
.BR \-\-pdf ,
.BR \-\-png ,
.BR \-\-play ,
.BR \-\-tune
or
.BR \-\-save ,
it does that and exits without opening one. Any other switch \(em a chain of
effects, a sampled instrument \(em opens the window with that rig already on
the part, rather than asking for it twice.
.PP
Drawing and rendering need a font database and not a screen. With neither
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
nor
.B DISPLAY
set, Qt's offscreen platform is used, so the command line half of the program
works over
.BR ssh (1).
.PP
Tracks are numbered from zero, in the order
.B \-\-info
prints them. More than one file may be named; each is read in turn.
.PP
Fretwork reads
.I .gp
and deliberately cannot write it. An imported score stays as its author wrote
it; what Fretwork saves, it saves as
.IR .fw .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Reading a score
.TP
.B \-\-info
Print what the file holds \(em title, artist, the version that wrote it, bars
notated and played, duration, tempo, and a line per track with its programme,
note count and tuning \(em and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-repeats
Read the score as notated rather than as played: repeats are not expanded and
each bar appears once.
.TP
.BI \-\-save " file.fw"
Convert the score to Fretwork's own format and write it there.
.SS Drawing it
.TP
.BI \-\-pdf " file.pdf"
Draw the tablature as a PDF, every page.
.TP
.BI \-\-png " file.png"
Draw one page of tablature as an image.
.TP
.BI \-\-page " number"
Which page
.B \-\-png
draws.
.TP
.BI \-\-track " number"
Which track to draw; the first by default. It also chooses the track
.B \-\-tune
tunes to, and where that track has no strings to tune \(em a drum kit, a
keyboard \(em the first track that has is used instead.
.SS Writing MIDI
.TP
.BI \-m " file.mid" "\fR, \fP" \-\-midi " file.mid"
Write the whole score as one MIDI file.
.TP
.BI \-\-stems " directory"
Write one MIDI file per track into a directory, named
.IR NN\-Track.mid ,
alongside a combined file named after the score. Where a technique cannot be
carried into MIDI \(em a bend across strings sharing one channel \(em what was
left behind is reported.
.SS Playing and rendering
.TP
.B \-\-play
Play it now, through the speakers.
.TP
.BI \-\-render " directory"
Render one WAV per track into a directory, named
.IR NN\-Track.wav ,
and a
.I mix.wav
of all of them.
.TP
.B \-\-dry
With
.BR \-\-render ,
write each effected part a second time as
.IR NN\-Track\-dry.wav ,
before its chain \(em so it can be reamped later. Only parts with a chain get
one.
.TP
.B \-\-click
Count it out: a metronome on every beat, played live or, with
.BR \-\-render ,
written as
.I click.wav
beside the stems. It is deliberately left out of
.IR mix.wav ,
because a click baked into a mix is one nobody can take out again.
.TP
.BI \-\-solo " track"
Hear only these tracks. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-mute " track"
Silence these tracks. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-audio\-driver " name"
Which FluidSynth audio driver to play through.
.B file
writes to disk instead of to a device.
.TP
.B \-\-ports
Give every track a pair of ports in the audio graph, for a DAW to record.
.TP
.B \-\-follow
Take the transport from the audio graph, so a DAW starts and locates it.
Implies
.BR \-\-ports .
.SS The instruments and the rig
.TP
.BI \-\-soundfont " file.sf2"
The SoundFont to play and render with.
.TP
.BI \-\-sfz " track=file.sfz"
Play a track from an SFZ instrument rather than a General MIDI programme.
Repeatable. Parts without one carry on as they were.
.TP
.BI \-\-lv2 " track=uri,uri"
Put a chain of LV2 effects on a track, nearest the instrument first \(em which
is where an amplifier stands, and in front of the fader, because behind it the
distortion would change every time somebody adjusted a level. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-knob " track:plugin:name=value"
Set one control on one plugin: the track, which plugin along the chain, the
name the plugin gives the control, and the value. Verbose because two
amplifiers in one chain both have a Drive. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-voicing " track:plugin=name"
Set a plugin in a chain to a guitarix preset. What a voicing cannot carry \(em
the factory bank leans on modules that ship no LV2 plugin \(em is named rather
than quietly dropped.
.TP
.B \-\-voicings
List the guitarix presets installed, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-effects
List the LV2 effects installed, with their port counts and URIs, and exit.
.SS Tuning
.TP
.B \-\-tune
Tune to this score, listening on an audio input: the targets are the file's own
tuning, capo included, rather than the nearest chromatic note. With no file
named, standard guitar tuning is used.
.TP
.BI \-\-input " name"
Which audio input to listen on; the desktop's own by default.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the options and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help\-all
Show the options including Qt's own, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-author
Show author information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-license
Show licence information and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-desktopfile " name"
The base file name of the desktop entry for this application.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything asked for was done.
.TP
.B 1
Something failed: a file that could not be read, a stem that could not be
written, a tuner that could not open an input, or a window that could not be
created. What failed is named on standard error.
.TP
.B 2
No file was named, and the switches given need one.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ", " DISPLAY
With neither set, Qt's offscreen platform is used and no window can open. The
drawing and rendering switches still work.
.TP
.B LV2_PATH
Where lilv looks for plugins, and therefore what
.B \-\-effects
finds.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/fretworkrc
Settings.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/fretworkstaterc
Window and dialogue state.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/fretwork/instruments
SFZ libraries found here are offered in the track panel, grouped by the library
they came in.
.TP
.I score.rig
The rig for a score \(em plugins by URI, knobs by the symbol the plugin
publishes, and samplers by path \(em kept beside the score rather than inside
it, because those are facts about one machine and the score is the part that
travels. The whole name and then the suffix:
.I Horses.gp
gets
.IR Horses.gp.rig .
Plain JSON. A score that has never been saved has nowhere to keep one yet.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print what a file holds:
.PP
.EX
fretwork \-\-info "Beautiful Losers.gp"
.EE
.PP
Play one track and nothing else:
.PP
.EX
fretwork "The Dogs Of War.gp" \-\-play \-\-solo 0
.EE
.PP
A sampled guitar, through its own amplifier and cabinet, out as its own stem,
with the unamplified take written beside it:
.PP
.EX
GX=http://guitarix.sourceforge.net/plugins
fretwork Horses.gp \-\-render out/ \-\-dry \e
    \-\-sfz 0=emily_clean.sfz \e
    \-\-lv2 "0=$GX/gx_amp#GUITARIX,$GX/gx_cabinet#CABINET" \e
    \-\-voicing "0:0=Iron Man"
.EE
.PP
Tune the low string to what this piece wants of it:
.PP
.EX
fretwork "Slomosa\-Horses.gp" \-\-tune
.EE
.SH LIMITATIONS
Slides, tremolo, natural and artificial harmonics, grace notes and trills are
read from the file but not yet translated into sound. Alternate endings and
jumps cannot be flattened into a played order, and a score containing them says
so rather than playing something adjacent. In MIDI output, the strings of one
track share a channel, so a bend moves every note that channel is holding \(em
a property of the MIDI file format and not of the program; rendered audio has
no such limit.
.PP
Per-track LV2 chains and SFZ sampling are present and experimental.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fluidsynth (1)
.PP
The project's own documentation, which argues rather than merely describes:
.UR https://github.com/SonicP3L1C4N/fretwork
.UE
.SH AUTHOR
Written by Gary Bissett.
.SH REPORTING BUGS
.UR https://github.com/SonicP3L1C4N/fretwork/issues
.UE
